{
  "case_id": "2011-11-159",
  "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Eric D. Miller, on behalf of the Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Social Security Administration has reasonably interpreted the Social Security Act to provide that, as a general rule, a person seeking to establish eligibility for child survivor benefits must show that he or she would have been able to inherit personal property from the decedent under applicable State intestacy law. That interpretation is supported by the text, structure, and history of the Act, and it comports with principles of federalism because it defers to State law on the determination of family status, which is a traditional subject of State regulation. It's reasonable and entitled to deference under Chevron."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: But what if they did -- what if they did that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Eric D. Miller, on behalf of the Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There -- there are two alternative mechanisms that Congress added to the statute in the 1960s to allow children who lack intestacy rights to establish their eligibility. Those are 416(h)(2)(B) and 416(h)(3). And 416(h)(3) says that an applicant who is the son or daughter of an insured individual but is not a child under -- under paragraph (A), which is the reference to State intestacy law, shall nevertheless be deemed to be a child if there was an acknowledgment in writing that the child was -- that the applicant was the son or daughter, or there was a court decree for support."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: There was in my memory, and it's been a while, that some States, if not all -- and that's what I was going to ask you -- basically deem any child born during the marriage to be a child of the marriage, whether it's a biological child or not, so that if a mother has had a relationship outside of marriage, the married parent's still responsible for that child."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If you were to take Respondents' approach, you would just stop there and apply some sort of Federal standard of figuring out whether people are married or not. But in fact 416(h), in paragraph (1) of 416(h), says an applicant is the wife or husband or widow or widower of an insured person if the State courts would regard them as being married."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, Mr. Miller."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One is whether all of the categories of applicants for child survivor benefits that are defined to be children in the statutory definition of \"child,\" section 416, qualify for child benefits without reference to State law, as we submit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "tell me what purpose 416(h) serves in this statute --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Rothfeld",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 0.92,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think that the question that the Court has to confront here is, Congress wrote a Federal definition of the word \"child.\" And it was -- sort of the first question in the case, I think, is whether we are correct in our understanding that, when Congress wrote this definition, all applicants for child survivor benefits who fall within that category, those defined categories, qualify."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question is what -- you haven't mentioned the text that suggests you're not right, which is right in (h) which says: In determining whether an applicant is a child of an insured, the Commissioner shall apply such law as intestacy law. Okay? That's what it says. Now, how do you get out of that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 0.97,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0002-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It says, \"Applicants who according to such law would have the same status relative to taking intestate property as a child or a parent shall be deemed such.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Reporter",
      "case_id": "2011-11-159",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "46081876c294b1139da270dd2715689383f7055eb59b8d382cbef713265a9ce7",
      "evidence_id": "ev-46081876c294-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
